\paragraph{CIFAR-10-C stress grid: the harmful regime (the decisive test).}
The per-corruption suite above is helpful-dominated by construction. To probe the
\emph{harmful} regime on the same standard benchmark we ran a \emph{pre-registered} stress
grid over the official CIFAR-10-C corruptions, crossing severity $\{1,5\}\times$ batch
$\{$large-iid $200$, small $16$, tiny $8\}\times$ composition $\{$iid, class-imbalanced,
single-class/label-shift$\}\times$ update $\{$mild $10$ steps; aggressive $50$ steps,
lr $2.5{\times}10^{-3}\}$, $2$ repeats ($432$ conditions/method). Benefit $B$ is measured on
a class-balanced held-out eval set so collapse is real accuracy loss, not a batch artifact;
\textsc{KGA} sees only label-free $Z$. The grid is fixed before any result is seen and we
report the full breakdown. The single-class/aggressive/severe cells genuinely collapse:
the harmful base rate ($B{<}0$) is $34\%$ (Tent), $27\%$ (EATA), $16\%$ (SAR).

On this mix \textsc{KGA} \textbf{beats both} trivial policies for Tent and EATA
(Table~\ref{tab:decisive}): regret-to-oracle $0.0016$/$0.0015$ versus always-adapt
